Divide by \(2\), rewrite, add \(\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^2=1\) to both sides, then take square roots:
\[
\begin{aligned}
x^2-2x-\frac{3}{2} &= 0\\
x^2-2x &= \frac{3}{2}\\
(x-1)^2 &= \frac{5}{2}\\
x-1 &= \pm\sqrt{\frac{5}{2}}=\pm\frac{\sqrt{10}}{2}\\
x &= 1\pm\frac{\sqrt{10}}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

Rewrite as \(x^2+3x=1\). Add \(\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^2=\frac{9}{4}\) to both sides, then take square roots:
\[
\begin{aligned}
x^2+3x+\frac{9}{4} &= 1+\frac{9}{4}=\frac{13}{4}\\
\left(x+\frac{3}{2}\right)^2 &= \frac{13}{4}\\
x+\frac{3}{2} &= \pm\frac{\sqrt{13}}{2}\\
x &= \frac{-3\pm\sqrt{13}}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
